  <h2>Is Saily or SimLocal the Right Pick</h2>
  <div class="decision-grid">
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--primary">
      <h3>Go with Saily if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You'd normally pay for a VPN/ad-blocker separately when traveling anyway</li>
        <li>You're crossing multiple countries and don't want a new eSIM at every border</li>
        <li>You don't need a real phone number, just data and security extras</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--secondary">
      <h3>Go with SimLocal if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You're heading to the UK and want a real phone number, not just data</li>
        <li>You want a no-questions refund policy if your plans change</li>
        <li>You're flying through Heathrow and would rather get in-person help than follow a support article</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Two Things Worth Knowing Before You Decide</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Saily's "unlimited" plans aren't uncapped.</strong> Its own fair-use policy gives full speed for the first 5GB in a rolling 24-hour window in most destinations (3GB in some), then throttles to 1Mbps until the local-midnight reset. Fine for normal phone use, worth knowing if several people share one hotspot. SimLocal doesn't sell unlimited plans at all; it's fixed-GB buckets with a comparatively short 14-day expiration window after purchase, so don't buy a SimLocal plan too far ahead of your trip.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Support channels work differently.</strong> Saily and SimLocal both skip the traditional phone line, but Saily leans on in-app/website support while SimLocal's 24/7 channel is specifically WhatsApp, plus a small footprint of staffed airport counters including Heathrow, something Saily has no equivalent of.
  </p>
  <p>
    For device setup and eSIM basics that apply to both providers, see our <a href="/compatible-devices/">eSIM compatible devices</a> guide. If a bundled number matters more than security extras but you don't need it to be specifically UK, see our <a href="/compare/airhub-vs-simlocal/">Airhub vs SimLocal comparison</a> for how SimLocal's number stacks up against a cheaper per-GB alternative instead.
  </p>

  <!-- FAQ -->
  <h2>Saily vs SimLocal FAQ</h2>
  <div class="content-faq">
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which is cheaper, Saily or SimLocal</h3>
      <p>Both sit in the "Cheap" price bracket on this site. Saily publishes fixed per-GB country pricing; SimLocal prices per destination and plan length at checkout instead of a flat per-GB rate, so there's no single number to compare directly. The bigger difference is usually the code: SimLocal's own ADAMANDLINDS code gives 10% off, while Saily's best public code gives 15% off storewide.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Does either come with a real phone number</h3>
      <p>SimLocal does, on most UK plans: a full UK number for calls and texts, not just data. Saily doesn't offer a phone number at all; it's data (plus the built-in VPN/ad-blocker) only.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which is better for a multi-country trip</h3>
      <p>Saily, generally. It sells one universal eSIM that keeps working as you cross into a new supported country, so there's no swapping profiles at each border the way a country-specific plan requires.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Can I get a refund if I change my mind</h3>
      <p>SimLocal's "No Quibble Refunds" policy means yes, for any reason, no explanation needed. Saily offers a full refund if the eSIM was never installed or activated within 30 days, or if less than 1% of the data was used, which is a narrower window than SimLocal's.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Is support available if something goes wrong mid-trip</h3>
      <p>Yes for both, but differently. SimLocal's 24/7 channel is specifically WhatsApp, plus a staffed counter at Heathrow. Saily supports through its app and website rather than a dedicated messaging app or physical location.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

